<!--
  Authoritative source: the client certificate Apigee itself received during
  the two-way TLS handshake on its own VirtualHost, when mutual auth
  terminates at the gateway. tls.client.raw.cert/.s.dn/.i.dn are only
  populated when the VirtualHost has ClientAuthEnabled + a TrustStore
  configured (Edge Private/Public Cloud) or
  propagateTLSInformation.clientProperties = true (Apigee X/hybrid); that is
  environment/VirtualHost config outside this bundle, not something a
  SharedFlowBundle can set.

  Only reached (see sharedflows/default.xml's Condition) when
  tls.client.raw.cert is confirmed non-null, so it is safe to use <Template>
  here (which, unlike <Ref>, always produces a string, even ""; running it
  unconditionally would make client.cert.base64 resolve to "" instead of
  staying null when TLS gave us nothing, which would wrongly skip the header
  fallback below).

  client.cert.base64 strips tls.client.raw.cert's PEM armor and internal
  line-wrap whitespace down to bare base64, since that (not full PEM) is
  what gets embedded in the request to ISAM. replaceAll's regex is Apigee's
  message-template function (Java regex under the hood, confirmed against a
  Dino Chiesa Apigee-MessageTemplate-Demo example using the same
  {replaceAll(source,'pattern','replacement')} form), not JavaScript/Java
  code; no JavaCallout needed for this.

  Deliberately split into 4 dead-simple steps chained through intermediate
  variables, rather than one combined regex, after two earlier single-regex
  attempts (with alternation via | and \s/\\s substitutions for the literal
  spaces) both still came back as unevaluated literal text in trace on a
  real environment (Apigee Edge Private Cloud 4.53.01); the two
  documented pitfalls (single- vs double-backslash for \s; no spaces
  anywhere in the expression, even inside a quoted literal) explained but
  did not actually fix, so something about combining alternation with those
  substitutions in one expression is still suspect on this version. Each
  step below removes exactly one literal substring or the \\s shorthand,
  alone, with zero alternation and zero embedded spaces; if this still
  fails, check client.cert.base64.step1/.step2/.step3 in trace to see
  exactly which single-purpose step is the one actually failing to
  evaluate, rather than guessing at the combined expression again.
-->
<AssignMessage name="AM-Extract-Client-Cert-TLS">
    <DisplayName>AM Extract Client Cert TLS</DisplayName>
    <AssignVariable>
        <Name>client.cert.base64.step1</Name>
        <Template>{replaceAll(tls.client.raw.cert,'-----BEGIN','')}</Template>
    </AssignVariable>
    <AssignVariable>
        <Name>client.cert.base64.step2</Name>
        <Template>{replaceAll(client.cert.base64.step1,'-----END','')}</Template>
    </AssignVariable>
    <AssignVariable>
        <Name>client.cert.base64.step3</Name>
        <Template>{replaceAll(client.cert.base64.step2,'CERTIFICATE-----','')}</Template>
    </AssignVariable>
    <AssignVariable>
        <Name>client.cert.base64</Name>
        <Template>{replaceAll(client.cert.base64.step3,'\\s','')}</Template>
    </AssignVariable>
    <AssignVariable>
        <Name>client.cert.subject.dn</Name>
        <Ref>tls.client.s.dn</Ref>
    </AssignVariable>
    <AssignVariable>
        <Name>client.cert.issuer.dn</Name>
        <Ref>tls.client.i.dn</Ref>
    </AssignVariable>
    <AssignVariable>
        <Name>client.cert.subject.dn.base64</Name>
        <Template>{encodeBase64(tls.client.s.dn)}</Template>
    </AssignVariable>
    <AssignVariable>
        <Name>client.cert.issuer.dn.base64</Name>
        <Template>{encodeBase64(tls.client.i.dn)}</Template>
    </AssignVariable>
    <IgnoreUnresolvedVariables>true</IgnoreUnresolvedVariables>
</AssignMessage>
